  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('chakabata')",
      "content": "The verb 'chakabata' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Colours & Describing Objects in Shona ('mutema')",
      "content": "This passage uses colour vocabulary such as 'mutema'. Shona has three traditional core colour adjectives that change their prefix slightly to match the noun they describe: '-chena' (white/clean, e.g., 'ruvara ruchena' or 'shangu chena'), '-tema / nhema' (black/dark, e.g., 'mota nhema' or 'ruvara rutema'), and '-tsvuku' (red/bright brown, e.g., 'mota tsvuku' or 'ruvara rutsvuku'). For other colours like green ('girinhi'), blue ('bhuruu'), or orange ('orenji'), speakers typically say 'ruvara rwe-' ('the colour of...') followed by the colour name, as in 'ruvara rwegirinhi' (green).",
      "rule": "Core Colours: -chena (white), -tema / nhema (black), -tsvuku (red) | Phrase pattern: ruvara rwe-[colour] (e.g., ruvara rwegirinhi)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Possessive Pronouns: Saying 'My', 'Your', and 'Our' ('rwake')",
      "content": "In Shona, possessive words like 'rwake' ('my/your/our/their') always follow the noun they belong to! The six core possessive stems are '-ngu' (my), '-ko' (your), '-ke' (his/her/its), '-du' (our), '-nyu' (your plural/polite), and '-vo' (their). A short linking sound ('wa-', 'ya-', 'ra-', 'cha-', 'dza-', 'ma-') attaches to the front of the stem to match the noun: for example, 'shamwari yangu' (my friend), 'munda wako' (your field), 'mabhuku edu' (our books), and 'kubudirira kwenyu' (your success).",
      "rule": "Noun + [Linking Prefix + -ngu (my) / -ko (your) / -ke (his/her) / -du (our) / -nyu (your polite) / -vo (their)]"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Tailoring, Craftwork ('Chigero') & Traditional Wrist Bangles ('Nhete')",
      "content": "Look at the word 'chigero' ('scissors', from 'kugera' = to cut/shear) alongside 'nhete' (slender metal or beaded wrist bangles). Dressmaking, hairdressing, and artisanal crafts are thriving small enterprises across Zimbabwean towns.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
